\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Visual anatomy of the six flagship order-four magic squares examined across Sections~\ref {sec:vectors}--\ref {sec:stacking}. (a)~D\"urer's associative square \#175 (1514, Group~III), whose antipodal pairs sum to $17$ across $O$, partitioning into $12$ outer hull vertices $V_0$ and $4$ inner core vertices $V_1$. (b)~Square \#70 (Group~VI), coloured by concentric rings $R_{\mathrm  {in}},R_{\mathrm  {mid}},R_{\mathrm  {out}}$, where parallel ring dipoles $\mathbf  d_{\mathrm  {out}}=5\mathbf  d_{\mathrm  {in}}$ achieve the Pell ratio $9/4\approx \sqrt  {5}$, and (c)~square \#45 ($\kappa =+9$, $\alpha ^*=-1$), whose single border swap $(8,9)\leftrightarrow (15,2)$ flips a heavy-core gem into hollow-cavity sibling \#54. (d)~Square \#46 (Group~VI), shaded by quadrant quadrupole $z_{ij}x_jy_i$, attaining the maximum $|Q_{xy}|=60$ and fastest $180^\circ $ Dzhanibekov flip about $y=-x$. (e)~Square \#673 (Group~VI), whose $8$-vertex U-cradle $V_0$ encloses an $8$-vertex inverted-T core $V_1$ ($\Delta \rho _1/\rho _0=5/4$), and (f)~square \#763 (Group~VI), whose centred knight's-move facets $\{13,14,15,16\}$ and $\{1,2,3,4\}$ lock into an area-$5$ mirror-twin tower.}}{3}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
