\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional rotational dynamics of Magic Gems. (a)~Universal inertia ratio $I_\perp (\lambda )/I_{zz}=\frac  12(1+\lambda ^2(n^2+1))$ versus vertical scale $\lambda $ for $n=3,4,5$, with 3D inertia ellipsoids at $\lambda _{\mathrm  {cube}}=1/5$ ($21/25$), the spherical-top transition $\lambda _{\mathrm  {iso}}=1/\sqrt  {17}$ ($1$), and $\lambda =1$ ($9$) for $n=4$. (b)~Triaxial Poinsot energy ellipsoid of entry-weighted square \#46 ($I_1=110<I_2=230<I_3=340$), showing the figure-eight separatrix and heteroclinic polhode connecting $+\omega _2$ to $-\omega _2$. (c)~Euler time series \eqref  {eq:euler}: an axisymmetric magic rotor precesses harmonically with constant $|\boldsymbol  \omega _\perp |$, whereas entry-weighted square \#46 ($|Q_{xy}|=60$) executes periodic $180^\circ $ Dzhanibekov flips between $+\omega _2$ and $-\omega _2$. (d)~Census of the quadrupole split $|Q_{xy}(S)|=\frac  12(I_2-I_1)$ across all $880$ order-four magic squares.}}{11}{figure.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:precession}{{4}{11}{Three-dimensional rotational dynamics of Magic Gems. (a)~Universal inertia ratio $I_\perp (\lambda )/I_{zz}=\frac 12(1+\lambda ^2(n^2+1))$ versus vertical scale $\lambda $ for $n=3,4,5$, with 3D inertia ellipsoids at $\lambda _{\mathrm {cube}}=1/5$ ($21/25$), the spherical-top transition $\lambda _{\mathrm {iso}}=1/\sqrt {17}$ ($1$), and $\lambda =1$ ($9$) for $n=4$. (b)~Triaxial Poinsot energy ellipsoid of entry-weighted square \#46 ($I_1=110<I_2=230<I_3=340$), showing the figure-eight separatrix and heteroclinic polhode connecting $+\omega _2$ to $-\omega _2$. (c)~Euler time series \eqref {eq:euler}: an axisymmetric magic rotor precesses harmonically with constant $|\boldsymbol \omega _\perp |$, whereas entry-weighted square \#46 ($|Q_{xy}|=60$) executes periodic $180^\circ $ Dzhanibekov flips between $+\omega _2$ and $-\omega _2$. (d)~Census of the quadrupole split $|Q_{xy}(S)|=\frac 12(I_2-I_1)$ across all $880$ order-four magic squares}{figure.41}{}}
